WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.683
Cognitive Load Theory helps designers build learning experiences that respect working memory limits.

2
00:00:05.683 --> 00:00:13.552
Learners can only process so much new information at once, especially when they lack schemas for the domain.

3
00:00:13.552 --> 00:00:22.732
Strong design supports the movement from limited working memory activity to organized long-term knowledge that learners can retrieve and use.

4
00:00:22.732 --> 00:00:27.541
A major takeaway is to distinguish necessary difficulty from unnecessary difficulty.

5
00:00:27.541 --> 00:00:35.410
Intrinsic load comes from the task itself and must be managed through sequencing, chunking, scaffolding, and progressive complexity.

6
00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:41.530
Extraneous load comes from confusing or inefficient instruction and should be reduced wherever possible.

7
00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:46.776
Productive mental effort should go toward understanding, schema building, and skill development.

8
00:00:46.776 --> 00:00:53.334
Worked examples, guided practice, feedback, signaling, purposeful multimedia, and adaptive support all help manage load.

9
00:00:53.334 --> 00:00:55.520
Beginners often need more structure.

10
00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:02.077
As learners gain expertise, guidance should fade and practice should become more varied and realistic.

11
00:01:02.077 --> 00:01:06.449
The design should evolve as the learner's knowledge changes.

12
00:01:06.449 --> 00:01:09.946
The goal is not to make learning effortless.

13
00:01:09.946 --> 00:01:12.132
Effort is part of learning.

14
00:01:12.132 --> 00:01:18.689
The goal is to make effort focused, productive, and connected to real understanding and performance.

15
00:01:18.689 --> 00:01:29.618
When instruction respects cognitive load, learners are more likely to see the important relationships, build usable schemas, and carry the skill back into real work.
